---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'China Visa Types Complete Guide 2026: Which Visa Do You Need?';
const description = 'A complete guide to Chinese visa types for tourists in 2026: visa-free, 240-hour transit, and L-type tourist visa.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="mb-12">
      <a href="/en/prepare/visa/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline inline-flex items-center gap-1 mb-4">← Visa & Entry</a>
      <div class="text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">Entry & Visa</div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        China Visa Types Complete Guide 2026: Which Visa Do You Need?
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <strong>Getting into China is easier than it was a few years ago.</strong>
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Depending on your nationality and travel plans, you might be able to enter China without a visa, take advantage of the 240-hour transit policy, or apply for a standard tourist visa. For most travelers, the process is straightforward once you figure out which option applies to your situation.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Do You Need a Visa to Visit China?</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <strong>Not everyone needs a traditional Chinese visa.</strong> There are currently three common ways to enter China:
      </p>

      <!-- Section 1: Visa-Free -->
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8 not-prose">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-brand-100 text-brand-700 text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</span>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 m-0">Visa-Free Entry</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          China currently grants visa-free entry to citizens of 50 countries for stays of up to 30 days. If your passport is from one of these countries, you can literally book a flight and walk through Chinese immigration without having applied for anything in advance. Just make sure your passport is valid for at least 6 months beyond your entry date, and you're good to go.
        </p>
          <a href="/en/prepare/visa/visa-free/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium hover:underline">
          China Visa-Free Countries List (2026) →
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 2: 240-Hour Transit -->
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8 not-prose">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-brand-100 text-brand-700 text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</span>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 m-0">240-Hour Visa-Free Transit</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Travelers from eligible countries can stay in China for up to 240 hours (10 days) while transiting to a third country or region (including Hong Kong, Macau, and Taiwan).
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          This option is popular with travelers making stopovers in cities like Shanghai, Beijing, Guangzhou, and Chengdu. <strong>Important note:</strong> This policy only applies to entries through international airports, not land border crossings.
        </p>
        <a href="/en/prepare/visa/transit-240/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium hover:underline">
          China 240-Hour Visa-Free Transit Explained →
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 3: Tourist Visa -->
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8 not-prose">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-brand-100 text-brand-700 text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</span>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 m-0">Tourist Visa (L-type)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If neither of the options above works for you, you'll typically need a Chinese L-type Tourist Visa.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The application process is generally straightforward and requires:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-gray-700 mb-4">
          <li>A valid passport</li>
          <li>A completed application form</li>
          <li>A recent photo</li>
          <li>Supporting documents (as required)</li>
        </ul>
        <a href="#" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium hover:underline">
          How to Apply for a Chinese Tourist Visa →
        </a>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">How Long Does It Take?</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Processing times vary by country and visa center. In most locations, standard processing takes 4–5 business days, though faster expedited services may be available for an additional fee.
      </p>



      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Which Option Is Right for You?</h2>

      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden mb-8 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-left">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900"></th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">30-Day Visa-Free</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">240-Hour Transit</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">L Tourist Visa</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700"><strong>Who qualifies</strong></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">50 eligible countries</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">55 eligible countries + transit route</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Everyone else</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700"><strong>Max stay</strong></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">30 days</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">10 days (240 hours)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">30-90 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700"><strong>Advance paperwork</strong></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">None (just show up)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 weeks</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700"><strong>Cost</strong></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Free</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Free</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">$30-$100 USD</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700"><strong>Flexibility</strong></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Travel anywhere</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Region-restricted</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Travel anywhere</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 mb-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is visa-free entry the same as a visa?</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>No.</strong> Visa-free entry means you don't need a visa stamp in your passport at all. You just show your passport to immigration and they let you in. It's completely different from having a visa — it's the absence of one.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to register with police after entering visa-free?</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">If you're staying at a hotel, they'll handle the registration automatically. If you're staying at a private residence, your host needs to register you at the local police station within 24 hours. This applies regardless of how you entered China.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">What if I have dual citizenship?</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">China doesn't recognize dual nationality. Use the passport you intend to enter China with. If one of your passports is from a visa-free country, use that one — it simplifies everything.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Before applying for a visa, check whether you qualify for visa-free entry or the 240-hour transit policy. If not, a standard L-type Tourist Visa remains the most common route for visitors.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
